We sincerely thank the reviewers for the time and effort they dedicated to evaluating our submission, as well as for their constructive and encouraging comments regarding our work on *subjective ability evaluation*, *persona modeling logic*, *behavioral consistency analysis*, and *virtual student agent design*.

The goal of **EduPersona** is to establish a systematic benchmark for subjective ability assessment, aimed at characterizing the capability boundaries of virtual student agents in subjective behavioral dimensions such as emotion perception, help-seeking strategies, cognitive-state expression, and multi-turn interaction stability. The main contributions of the work are three-fold:

* **We define subjective ability boundaries for educational behaviors and construct a multidimensional evaluation framework** covering emotion understanding, help-seeking, interaction style, and cognitive biases;
* **We build a cross-topic, cross-task benchmark for subjective behavioral assessment**, incorporating IRF–BEC structures, persona-expression consistency, multi-turn stability, and structural bias analysis, providing a systematic validation pathway for subjective capabilities in virtual student agents;
* **We introduce a human–AI alignment perspective through human–model consistency evaluation**, quantifying the upper bound of human agreement via expert assessment and analyzing alignment between experts and GPT-4o, thereby validating the stability and absence of significant structural bias in the automatic evaluation module across different model families and persona types.

This work aims to construct a benchmark for subjective ability evaluation, focusing on defining the subjective behavioral capability boundaries of virtual students, rather than developing an applied teaching system or directly validating downstream instructional effectiveness. However, several comments approached the work from the perspective of applied systems or post-training evaluation, raising expectations related to external benchmarks, circularity, or cross-persona comparisons, positions that fall outside the evaluation layer that EduPersona targets. 

To address these conceptual differences, we clarified and supplemented the rebuttal and revised manuscript with:

* Systematic presentation of inter-expert agreement and alignment results between GPT-4o and human experts on subjective tasks;
* Additional explanation of acceptance rates and robustness of the automatic evaluator across different model families and ten persona types, clarifying its reliability boundary in subjective evaluation scenarios;
* Clearer definition of persona-label origins, behavioral-consistency measures, and their theoretical grounding, further strengthening the conceptual and operational framing of “subjective ability boundaries.”

At the same time, due to the large-scale anonymity breach that occurred at ICLR this year, the conference implemented special policies to safeguard fairness in the review process, preventing further author–reviewer clarification in later stages. **Within this objective constraint, we recognize that the limited communication space in the current mechanism makes it difficult for a benchmark-oriented work such as this one to have its structural contributions and evidence chain fully presented and fairly evaluated.** Out of respect for scientific rigor and the review process, we have decided to voluntarily withdraw the submission. We will continue to refine the manuscript based on the reviewers’ comments and rebuttal clarifications, and later submit to a venue more suitable for presenting research of this nature.
